<p class="copy">
	Skiff, tender, dinghy, lighter—these small craft are the basic utility vessels of large stations and ships, carrying goods and people between distant sections, often leaving the vessel entirely to skirt its exterior. Most common in older stations without sufficient internal transportation networks, or in particularly diffuse stations with many smaller substations, a skiff is often the simplest way to get from one point to another without donning a voidsuit.
</p>
<p class="copy">
	Typically carrying only a few passengers and their pilot, skiffs are built for maneuverability rather than speed or range. Small liquid rocket boosters are common, though some use robotic limbs to push and pull themselves through the often cramped confines of a station's voidspace. Skiffs rarely venture beyond the vessel or station they serve.
</p>
